"""Buyer-facing editorial content for packaging product and guide pages."""
PROFILES={
'rigid':[
('Presentation and opening experience','A presentation box should reveal the product in a deliberate order. Begin with the complete set: bottles, jars, accessories, printed inserts and any protective wrapping. Decide which item the buyer sees first and how it is lifted out. A close-fitting insert can organize the contents, but finger access and comfortable removal matter as much as the appearance of the closed box. Consider the retail shelf, gift handover and repeated opening as separate moments in the design.'),
('Board, wrap and insert selection','Review the board structure and the decorative outer wrap separately. The wrap determines much of the printed appearance, while the assembled construction determines the shape and opening. Request an insert layout around actual product dimensions, including caps, shoulders and irregular edges. Foam, paperboard, molded pulp or fabric-covered directions should be discussed against the reference options and the required presentation. Do not select an insert purely by color: retention, contact surfaces, removal and assembly need a physical check.'),
('Closure and assembly','Magnetic, drawer and lift-off constructions create different packing operations. On a magnetic format, check flap alignment and how the lid settles when loaded. On a drawer, check sliding clearance and the pull position. On a lift-off lid, review overlap and access. If a collapsible direction is requested, compare its assembly steps and packed dimensions with the assembled version. Flat supply should be evaluated using actual export-carton measurements rather than an assumed freight saving.'),
('Artwork across the opening sequence','Plan graphics for the lid, side panels, inside cover and insert as one system. Keep critical logos away from wrap turns, joints and moving edges. Separate foil, embossing and spot-coating artwork from the process-color design so their position can be reviewed. A flat visual can explain the graphic hierarchy, while a formed sample answers questions about alignment, opening and how different surfaces meet. Inspect fine text and contrast at the size the recipient will actually hold.')],
'mailer':[
('Design around the packed parcel','A mailer is a complete parcel system, not only an outer box. Measure the product together with its retail pack, insert, wrapping and any accessories. Describe the way the order travels: packing bench, courier handling, returns and final opening. Too much clearance can allow movement; too little can make packing slow or force pressure onto the contents. Review a loaded structure before adding decorative finishing, especially for glass, coated products and items with projecting corners.'),
('Board and closure decisions','Specify the corrugated construction, finished internal dimensions and closure together. A flute description alone does not establish the performance of the finished parcel. Locking tabs, glue areas, tape or a tear strip influence how the box is packed and opened. For a return journey, discuss whether the customer needs a second closure or another shipping carton. The selected board and closure should be evaluated with the actual packed weight and expected handling conditions.'),
('Printing for shipping and unboxing','Compare the outside shipping surface with the inside presentation surface. Shipping labels, tape and courier marks may cover exterior artwork, while the inside lid offers a cleaner brand moment. Leave practical space for delivery labels and avoid placing essential messages across folds, tears or closure points. On a natural kraft surface, review the artwork against the selected board shade. A screen preview cannot show every interaction between ink, liner texture and the finished folded structure.'),
('Parcel sample review','Pack the sample using the planned insert, closure and working method. Check whether the product moves, whether tabs remain seated and whether opening damages printed information. Photograph the complete arrangement before and after the agreed checks. Transport testing should match the distribution route and the product/package combination; a tidy sample photograph is not a transit-performance result. Keep the approved packing method with the dieline so later changes in filler or product size are not overlooked.')],
'carton':[
('Retail fit and product information','A folding carton brings structure and product information into a small space. Start with the complete product, including its cap, sleeve, label and any protective bag. Decide the display orientation and the panel that will face the shopper. Reserve room for the product name, variant, instructions and codes before filling the dieline with decoration. The carton should be easy to assemble, close and reopen during normal handling without forcing the contents against a flap or seam.'),
('Board, tuck and base selection','Choose paperboard together with the carton structure. Straight-tuck, reverse-tuck, self-lock and other base directions create different assembly and opening routines. Review the load at the base, the number of packing steps and the way the product is removed. Board weight is only part of the specification: surface, thickness, grain, scores and glue areas also need review. Ask for a structural sample sized around the real contents before treating a nominal dimension as production-ready.'),
('Print and finish planning','Place the main brand panel, side information and secondary copy on the current dieline. Separate foil, embossing and selected coating layers from the color artwork. Leave the agreed clear areas around folds, glue flaps and codes; the prepress team should confirm the required tolerances for the chosen route. Dark solids and pale colors can behave differently across paper surfaces. Approve print and finishing using a reference suitable for the actual material rather than a screen image alone.'),
('Assembly and shelf review','Use the sample to check closure engagement, panel alignment and how a row of cartons looks together. Test the packing sequence with the people or equipment that will fill the cartons. If a bottle or jar can shift, review the dimensions or an internal support instead of simply making the board heavier. Check readability after the carton is formed. Record revisions to both the dieline and artwork so a corrected label panel is not placed on an obsolete structural file.')],
'bag':[
('Size the bag around the purchase','Plan a paper bag around the complete retail purchase, including its product box, tissue and any additional items. Measure the height that remains below the handles and the depth needed at the base. A bag can appear large enough in a flat drawing yet be awkward to carry once filled. Consider the handover at the counter, a short walk and storage before use. Confirm the usable opening and the loaded shape with the intended retail purchase before approving the dimensions.'),
('Paper, handles and reinforcement','Paper surface, handle style, attachment and base construction work together. Review the intended load and the points where the contents contact the bag. A narrow bottle, a sharp carton corner and a soft garment create different demands. Discuss reinforcement at the handle and base when relevant, and inspect the assembled sample with the intended contents. Do not derive a carrying limit from paper weight alone; the complete bag and the agreed use conditions need their own review.'),
('Print coverage and coordinated branding','A paper bag often sits beside a box, card or label from the same range. Identify the most important shared brand color and compare samples together. Natural paper shade, coated surfaces and printed coverage can create different appearances from the same digital artwork. Review logos on both faces, gussets, handle positions and the base. Keep small text away from folds and handle openings, and confirm how foil or embossing will appear on the selected paper.'),
('Check the filled bag','Inspect seam bonding, the base fold, handle comfort and the way the loaded bag stands. Test the intended lifting, handover and opening routine with the actual purchase. Check access below the handles, support at the base and contact with corners of the packed item. Record the sample construction and load used for evaluation. Changes to dimensions, paper, handles or adhesive can require a new review.')],
'poly':[
('Choose the bag for its actual job','Garment protection, retail carrying and parcel shipping are different jobs. State whether the bag will be displayed, stored, opened repeatedly or used as an outer shipping layer. Include the folded product size and any items placed alongside it. The usable interior depends on the closure and seal areas as well as the flat bag dimensions. Review clarity, opacity and the packing experience with a physical sample before selecting a printed design.'),
('Film and closure specification','Define the material direction, thickness convention, finished size and closure in one specification. Zippers, adhesive flaps, hang holes and ventilation features should be reviewed where relevant to the product and distribution. A film name alone does not establish puncture, seal or carrying performance. Describe the actual contents and handling conditions so the sample check can target the right risks. Keep material and environmental claims tied to the structure and destination rather than to a general appearance.'),
('Printed branding on flexible surfaces','Flexible film changes shape when it is folded, filled and handled. Position the logo and important text where they remain readable on the packed bag. Clear and translucent films may require a white-ink decision; opaque surfaces need their own color reference. Keep artwork away from seal areas and the working parts of the closure. Review a filled sample for readability, opacity and scuffing against the actual garment or product instead of approving only a flat visual.'),
('Packing and opening trials','Try the intended packing sequence using the real contents. Check whether the product slides in cleanly, whether the closure has enough working space and whether any sharp edge stresses the film. For a resealable direction, inspect opening and reclosing under the expected use. For a mailer, review the full parcel arrangement and label position. Record the material, size and closure used in the trial so a later design change does not silently alter the approved packaging.')],
'pouch':[
('Begin with the product and filling route','Pouch selection begins with the contents, fill quantity, storage conditions and filling method. Whole beans, powders and irregular snacks occupy different volumes at the same weight. Describe any sensitivity to oxygen, moisture, light or aroma as separate requirements. The stand-up shape is only one part of the specification. A filled trial helps establish headspace, zipper clearance and the seal area before artwork and a material structure are approved.'),
('Material and functional features','Review the proposed layer structure and thickness with the intended product. A zipper, valve, window, spout or hang hole changes how the pouch is used and manufactured, so include these features in the brief from the beginning. A metallic appearance does not establish a complete barrier specification. Request relevant material documentation and agree the checks needed for the actual application. Product shelf life and direct-contact suitability require evidence for the selected structure and intended conditions.'),
('Artwork around seals and gussets','Map the artwork onto the agreed flat dimensions, gussets and sealing areas. Identify the front panel, back information panel and any surface that becomes less visible after filling. Confirm white-ink coverage for clear or metallic materials and keep variable information readable. Important copy should remain clear of the agreed seal and trimming tolerances. Review the printed result together with the filled shape, because a flat proof cannot show every fold or the final shelf presentation.'),
('Filled-pack validation','Use the actual product or an agreed representative fill to evaluate capacity, standing behavior and closure access. Review seal conditions with the filling process rather than assuming a stock sample proves the final pack. Check for product trapped in the seal and confirm how the customer opens and recloses the pouch. Keep the approved material, dimensions and feature set together in the specification. Any change in filling conditions, contents or laminate needs an appropriate review before repeat production.')],
'label':[
('Match the label to the application surface','Start with the surface the label will touch: glass, a coated carton, plastic film or another substrate. Describe its shape, texture and condition during application. Refrigeration, moisture, handling and the required removal behavior can change the adhesive brief. A label that looks correct on its backing sheet can behave differently on the actual product. Trial the chosen material and adhesive on representative finished surfaces before approving the full quantity.'),
('Shape, backing and application method','Specify the finished shape and dimensions together with sheet or roll supply. For rolls, include the applicator requirements, core, outer diameter and unwind drawing. For sheets, review spacing and ease of lifting each label. Die-cut artwork needs an agreed cut line and safe text area. Keep details away from the edge allowance, especially on small labels. If a seal bridges two surfaces, confirm the opening motion and contact area rather than selecting adhesive by appearance alone.'),
('Color, white ink and readable codes','Clear, white, metallic and textured label materials need different print decisions. On a clear label, the underlying container and product influence the result; discuss the white-ink layer before judging the color. Preserve the required space around barcodes or QR codes and check them on the formed, labelled product. Small text and pale colors should be reviewed at actual size. Decorative finishes should support readability rather than interfere with the information the buyer must scan or read.'),
('Application trial and approval','Apply samples using the planned hand or machine process. Inspect alignment, edge lifting, bubbles, dispensing and readability after the agreed exposure conditions. A roll that feeds in the wrong direction can be unusable even when its print is correct, so keep the approved unwind diagram with the artwork. Record material, adhesive, size and application conditions. Recheck the label if the container surface, coating or application method changes.')],
'print':[
('Define the job of the printed piece','A card, hang tag or booklet should make one next step easier: understanding the product, reading care instructions, identifying a variant or presenting a gift. Decide whether the buyer reads it before purchase, during unboxing or later in use. That decision guides finished size, paper, fold and copy hierarchy. Avoid squeezing every message onto a small face; a clear front and a more detailed reverse can be easier to use than one crowded design.'),
('Paper, attachment and finishing','Choose the paper surface and thickness against handling, print and attachment. For a hang tag, specify the hole position, string and how the tag hangs on the product. For an insert, check fit inside the complete package. For folded pieces, review panel order and how the reader opens them. Foil, embossing and die cutting can add emphasis, but fine details need a suitable artwork and sample review. Keep practical information readable after the piece is attached or folded.'),
('Artwork and information hierarchy','Prepare final wording before arranging the layout. Make product names, instructions and important care information easy to locate. Review text at actual size, including the smallest line and any QR code. Keep cut, fold, hole and finishing layers clearly identified in the artwork. If the printed piece is part of a coordinated packaging range, compare its paper and print reference alongside the box, bag or label rather than approving each in isolation.'),
('Approve the assembled context','Place the sample in its actual setting: inside a pack, attached to a product or on a counter. Check that the important printed face is visible and that handling is practical. Where folding or attachment is specified, review its position, sequence and effect on readable information. Confirm wording, translations and codes with the product owner before production. Keep the approved file and material reference together so repeat orders do not accidentally mix old copy with a new layout.')],
'display':[
('Design the display around replenishment','A cardboard display must work for the product, retail location and the person who replenishes it. Provide product dimensions, weight, quantity per shelf and the required footprint. Decide whether the display ships filled or is assembled in store. Review viewing height, access and how the last few products remain visible. A promotional image cannot establish shelf load or stability, so treat the loaded structure as a separate design and sample decision.'),
('Structure and support','Discuss the base, shelves, dividers, header and any internal support as one assembly. Concentrated product weight can stress a shelf differently from an evenly distributed load. Consider the floor or counter surface and the way the unit is moved. Agree assembly steps and the parts that need clear identification. Material and construction should be reviewed with the planned load and use period; a generic board description does not establish a display capacity.'),
('Graphics for the retail setting','Separate the headline message from product information and assembly instructions. A shopper should understand the category and offer from the intended viewing distance, while staff need practical loading and replenishment guidance. Keep critical copy away from folds, slots and hidden joins. Review the header with the assembled display and stocked products in place. Coordinated colors on shelves and side panels can support recognition without making every surface equally busy.'),
('Loaded sample review','Assemble the sample using the planned instructions and load it with representative products. Check shelf fit, access, stability, replenishment and the final footprint. If the display ships filled, review the outer shipping arrangement and protection as well. Record the construction, product load and conditions used in the evaluation. Changes to the product set, shelf quantity or structural parts should trigger an appropriate review before the display is repeated.')]
}

# Format-specific copy follows the reviewed product type, not supplier keyword order.
FLEXIBLE_MATERIAL=('Specify the complete film construction','A named film is a starting reference, not a complete converted-pack specification. Confirm layer identities, layer order, total thickness and how thickness is measured. Review the actual contents, contact conditions, storage and filling process together. If barrier performance, chemical compatibility or a shelf-life target matters, agree the relevant evidence for that exact structure. The metallic appearance of a printed surface does not identify an aluminum layer, and the same artwork can be printed on different constructions.')
FLEXIBLE_ARTWORK=('Plan artwork on the production drawing','Position the front design, product information and variable printing on the current conversion drawing. Identify every seal, trimming allowance, fold and opening feature that affects usable print space. Review white-ink coverage when clear or metallic-looking surfaces form part of the design. Check text and codes at finished size and with representative contents in the pack. Keep the approved artwork revision tied to its dimensions and construction so a later structural change does not silently move important information.')
PROFILES['pouch']=[
('Review the bottom-gusset format','This stand-up direction uses a bottom-gusset reference. Confirm the finished width, height, gusset convention and usable opening with the actual product. Standing behavior depends on fill distribution and the converted structure, so review the filled footprint and headspace before approving the dimensions. Include any listed zipper, window or hang feature in that review. A printed weight on the example artwork does not set the capacity of a different product or the final pouch.'),
FLEXIBLE_MATERIAL,FLEXIBLE_ARTWORK,
('Check filling, standing and opening','Run a representative filling trial and inspect the bottom fold, top seal area and access to the contents. Check that filling leaves the working clearance required by the selected opening. Where a reclosure is specified, evaluate it separately from the final production seal. Review the filled pack on the intended shelf and in its secondary carton. Record the contents, fill quantity and sample revision used for the check before carrying those decisions into a repeat order.')]
FORMAT_PROFILE_COPY={
'pouch-flat-bottom':[
('Define the base and panel geometry','Review the flat-bottom reference as a base, front, back and side-panel system. Confirm which dimensions describe the finished footprint and which describe the unfilled drawing. The same nominal fill mass can require different space for powders, grains or irregular pieces. Check the actual filled shape, the top opening and room for closing before choosing the final size. A base illustrated in a photograph does not establish the exact dimensions or fill capacity for your project.'),
('Check the formed pack and packing line','Fill a matching sample and inspect the base corners, side folds and upper closing area. Review panel alignment, access to any zipper and the way packs sit beside each other on the shelf. Try the planned filling and case-packing routine, including the number and orientation of packs per carton. Confirm that the printed information remains visible on the formed panels. Record the agreed construction and filled dimensions rather than approving only the empty bag.')],
'pouch-side-gusset':[
('Size the side folds around the fill','For this gusseted bag direction, distinguish face width, expanded gusset depth and overall length. Confirm the seam location, bottom conversion and usable top sealing area on a drawing. Review how the intended product distributes into the side folds, and decide how the filled top will be closed. A photograph of a standing bag does not by itself specify a flat-bottom construction. Use the stated fold geometry as a reference to confirm with a matching sample.'),
('Review folding, closing and case packing','Fill the sample through the intended equipment or packing method. Check that the side folds open consistently and that the top has the working space required for the selected closure. Inspect product movement, the folded upper section and the orientation used in the secondary carton. If a valve or reclosure is requested, specify it separately rather than assuming it from the coffee-style appearance. Keep the approved fold, seam and closure details with the size drawing.')],
'pouch-spout':[
('Match the fitment to filling and dispensing','Specify the fitment bore, position, cap and any sealing component together with the pouch drawing. Describe the actual formulation, fill volume, viscosity, particles and filling temperature before choosing the assembly. State whether the pack is filled through the fitment or through an open seam. A pictured cap does not establish its thread, bore or compatibility. Compare the intended pouring or squeezing action with a matching sample and document the configuration that will be supplied.'),
('Review the filled pouch and cap assembly','Trial the proposed filling route with the intended formulation or an agreed representative liquid. Inspect the fitment seal, cap engagement, filled handling and dispensing under the conditions agreed for the project. Confirm how the final pack is closed and transported. Do not treat a general photograph as evidence for hot filling, retort or repeated reuse. Keep the formulation, film, cap and fitment revision together when approving the sample or changing a repeat order.')],
'pouch-spout-dry':[
('Size the outlet for the dry contents','This dispensing direction concerns a dry product rather than an assumed liquid formula. Specify the particle size, dustiness, bulk behavior and target fill mass before choosing the fitment bore and cap. Review whether the contents can bridge at the outlet or collect in folds during pouring. Confirm the fill route and the fitment position with a drawing. A large round fitting in an illustration does not establish that one opening suits rice, litter and powders alike.'),
('Trial pouring and reclosure with a filled sample','Fill a matching pack with the intended dry contents. Review the pouring rate, outlet access, trapped material around the fitting and the way the cap closes after use. Inspect the seams and handle, where specified, under the agreed packed weight and handling routine. Record mass separately from package dimensions and nominal volume. Confirm the approved film, fitting and cap together; a change in particle size or formulation can require another filled-pack review.')],
'pouch-flat':[
('Confirm the flat pack and seal arrangement','Review the flat bag or pouch as a defined usable area bounded by its seals and opening. Specify the finished width and length, seal pattern, insertion direction and any requested tear or reclosure feature. Do not add a bottom-gusset or standing requirement unless it is part of the approved construction. Check the actual item, portion or sample in a matching pack. A flat presentation image does not identify every seam or prove a fixed capacity.'),
('Check insertion, sealing and user access','Try placing the intended contents in the sample using the planned packing routine. Check clearance at the opening and the working space required to close the filled pack. Inspect any sharp edges or bulky corners that contact the film. Review the intended opening motion and access to the contents without assuming repeated use or a zipper. Keep the agreed seal pattern, opening and material with the artwork and confirm the finished unit dimensions before ordering.')],
'pouch-specialty':[
('Define the actual converted format','Use this reference to identify the construction you need rather than assuming a standard doypack. Confirm the outline, seams, any folds, opening and intended contents on a drawing. For a shaped pack, include the usable fill area and clearance near narrow corners. For a storage or refill direction, confirm the actual closing and dispensing method. The photograph may illustrate a presentation idea or several variants, so identify one configuration for the quotation and sample review.'),
('Review the reference as a complete pack','Use a matching physical sample to evaluate the chosen construction with its intended contents. Check the packing sequence, closing area, access and handling rather than relying on a printed claim in the illustration. Define which features will be included and which are proposed changes. Keep the agreed material, dimensions and opening together in the specification. Where the image leaves the base or seam arrangement unclear, resolve that question before approving the production drawing.')],
'pouch-drink':[
('Specify the straw opening and final closure','The beverage reference shows straw access and top finger openings rather than establishing a screw-cap fitment. Confirm the straw insertion point, usable mouth, top sealing arrangement and any reclosure separately. Define the actual beverage, fill volume, filling route and intended holding conditions. Check the straw and filled pack together, including how the pack is carried. The appearance of a zipper-like top edge does not prove its closing method or liquid-retention performance.'),
('Trial filling, carrying and drinking','Fill a matching sample and follow the intended customer sequence: carrying the pack, inserting the straw, holding it and drinking. Inspect the top closing area and any handle openings with the filled weight. Agree the checks for the actual beverage and distribution conditions before approving the pack. Do not substitute cap torque or spout-thread checks for this opening style. Record the straw, top closure, dimensions and filling method as part of the approved specification.')],
}
for key,parts in FORMAT_PROFILE_COPY.items():PROFILES[key]=[parts[0],FLEXIBLE_MATERIAL,FLEXIBLE_ARTWORK,parts[1]]
PROFILES.update({
'paper-mailer':[
('Plan the envelope around the parcel','Measure the complete product or retail pack before selecting the mailing-bag width, length and any gusset. Include the space needed to insert the contents and fold the closing flap. Review the packed corners and the route through the packing bench, label application and delivery network. This is a parcel-envelope direction rather than a paper carrier with handles. Confirm the usable interior and the final sealing area with a sample containing the actual goods.'),
('Specify paper, seams and closure','Review the paper construction, seam layout and closing strip together. State any opening strip or return-flap requirement separately and confirm it on the drawing. A kraft appearance or paper weight alone does not establish parcel protection or resistance to moisture. Use the expected packed weight, product edges and delivery conditions to define suitable checks. Confirm the actual construction rather than treating a supplier bag-type label as proof of how the envelope will be converted.'),
('Leave room for shipping information','Position the brand artwork around the planned delivery label, closing flap and opening area. Check that folds and seams do not obscure essential printed information. Review the ink against the selected paper surface and agree any finishing that affects folding or sealing. If the mailer will be used for returns, identify which information must remain readable after opening. Evaluate the formed and labelled parcel rather than approving a flat front design alone.'),
('Trial the packed mailing routine','Pack and close a matching sample using the intended method. Review clearance, seam stress, the closing strip and the way the recipient opens the parcel. Inspect contact with product corners and any additional protective layer included in the order. Agree handling or transit checks around the actual route. Keep the paper, bag dimensions, closure and packed-product arrangement together in the approval record so changes to the contents are reviewed before another production run.')],
'poly-mailer':[
('Define the complete shipping envelope','A poly mailer is an outer parcel layer for a specified product and packing arrangement. Measure the folded goods, retail pack and any protective layer before setting the finished width and length. Include working space for the closing flap and planned delivery label. Confirm the usable opening and insertion method on a sample. The appearance of a branded mailing envelope does not establish its film thickness, puncture performance or protection for a different packed product.'),
('Review film, seams and adhesive flap','Specify opacity, film construction, thickness convention and seam arrangement together with the closing strip. If a return closure or opening strip is requested, show it on the drawing. Try the actual packed corners against the film and review the flap after sealing. Avoid treating a general material name as a performance result. Keep any material or environmental statement tied to documents for the agreed construction rather than to claims printed on the reference image.'),
('Plan print around labels and opening','Reserve space for address labels, carrier marks, the closing flap and any opening strip. Confirm the artwork orientation after the envelope is packed and folded. Review contrast and opacity where the goods could show through the film. Keep important brand and product information clear of seams and areas that will be covered during shipment. Inspect a formed, labelled sample using the actual packing method before approving print placement and finishing.'),
('Test packing and delivery handling','Use representative goods to trial insertion, closure and customer opening. Check film contact with corners, seam loading and whether the closing strip remains accessible to the packing team. Agree the relevant delivery or handling checks for the complete parcel rather than for an empty bag. Document the material, dimensions, closure and contents used in the trial. Revisit those checks when a product, protective layer or bag construction changes in a later order.')],
'carry-box':[
('Treat the handle as part of the box','Review this reference as a box with a carrying feature rather than as a flexible shopping bag. Measure the full contents and confirm the box footprint, height, opening and handle location on a drawing. Include any insert, wrapping or protective retail pack. Check how the loaded box is lifted, handed over and opened. A visible rope or ribbon handle does not identify its attachment strength or establish the carrying limit of the finished construction.'),
('Review board and handle attachment','Specify the board construction, folds, seams and handle fixing together. Inspect the load path between the handle, top panels and base with the actual product in place. Discuss reinforcement where the agreed load or geometry calls for it. Confirm whether the box is supplied assembled or flat and review the required packing steps. Keep structural decisions separate from decorative finishes so a printed appearance is not mistaken for evidence of load-bearing performance.'),
('Coordinate graphics with the opening','Plan the front, back and handle-facing panels on the current dieline. Keep important information clear of handle holes, folds, joins and moving lid edges. Confirm how the artwork looks when the box is carried and when it is placed on a counter. Review any requested foil, embossing or coating against the actual paper surface. A formed sample helps compare printed alignment and access in a way a flat image cannot.'),
('Approve the loaded carrying sample','Pack a sample with the complete contents and follow the intended lifting and handover routine. Inspect handle comfort, attachment, base support, closure and product movement under the agreed conditions. Check that opening the box remains practical after carrying. Record the exact construction, contents and load used for approval. A change in dimensions, handle or contents can affect that result, so keep the approval with the drawing and material specification for repeat orders.')],
'pillow':[
('Review the curved-end folding shape','A pillow-box direction uses curved closing flaps to create its formed volume. Specify the flat dieline dimensions separately from the usable interior after the ends are folded. Check the actual product, any inner wrapping and the space needed to insert and remove it. This reference should not be treated as a rigid magnetic or drawer box. Confirm the side seam, end geometry and intended closing routine with a sample before arranging the final artwork.'),
('Match paper and scores to the contents','Review paperboard, grain, scores and the glue seam together. The board needs to form around the selected curved shape without forcing the product against the end flaps. Confirm the intended load, storage and handling rather than assuming protection from the decorative appearance. If an inner support is needed, review its fit as a separate component. Keep the agreed paper and dieline with the structural sample so later artwork changes use the same construction.'),
('Place artwork around the curved folds','Map the front and back design onto the approved pillow-box drawing. Allow for the parts of the panel that curve or become less visible when the ends are closed. Keep text and codes away from the agreed score, seam and trimming allowances. Review any requested finish at the formed size and compare important colors on the actual material. A flat mockup is useful for layout, while a folded sample shows how the graphics read in hand.'),
('Check folding, packing and reopening','Assemble the sample using the intended packing method and place the real product inside. Review flap engagement, end alignment, product access and the way the closed pack sits in any secondary packaging. Inspect the printed surfaces after the expected handling routine. Record the usable dimensions and approved closing method rather than relying on an unqualified size from a photograph. Confirm the final paper, finish and drawing revision together before placing a custom order.')],
'food-box':[
('Define the role of the paper food pack','Confirm whether this reference is an outer presentation box, a carton around a wrapped product or a pack intended for direct food contact. Those roles require different specifications and supporting information. Describe the actual food, any inner tray or liner, fill quantity, packing temperature and delivery conditions. Review the complete packed arrangement with a sample. A photograph of food inside a box does not establish that every displayed material belongs in direct contact with it.'),
('Review structure, liners and closure','Specify the paperboard construction, usable interior, closing method and any separate tray, divider or liner. Check the load at the base and the clearance needed to fill and close the pack. Discuss grease, moisture and temperature requirements only for the actual food and agreed use conditions. Confirm relevant material information for that construction and destination. Do not infer these properties from a coating, window or paper shade visible in the illustration.'),
('Keep product information readable','Reserve room for the product name, variant, required information and codes on the approved drawing. Review how the pack will be displayed and whether a sleeve, liner or outer shipping carton covers any printed panel. Keep important copy away from folds, seams and closing areas. Approve requested print and finishing on a suitable material reference, and check the formed sample with representative contents rather than judging only an empty flat design.'),
('Trial the actual packing and delivery routine','Pack the sample with the proposed food arrangement, including every inner component. Review insertion, closure, access and movement under the conditions agreed for the project. For display packs, check shelf presentation; for delivery packs, agree handling or transit checks for the complete system. Keep direct-contact requirements and supporting documents attached to the approved material. Record changes to the product, liner or packing temperature before repeating production under a previous specification.')],
'carry-bag':[
('Identify the carrying and presentation format','Confirm the bag construction, opening and any handle or drawstring around the item it will carry. Include inner wrapping, product dimensions and the required access at packing and handover. This reference belongs to a gift or carrying task rather than an assumed food-filling pouch. Where the photograph does not establish the exact material, identify that choice before quoting. A matching physical sample helps resolve the seam, base and carrying arrangement.'),
('Match material and construction to use','Choose the actual fabric, paper or film direction together with its seams and attachment details. For a drawstring, confirm the cord path and closing action; for a handle, inspect the attachment and loaded clearance. State the intended load and handling conditions so the sample review targets the right construction. A photograph does not identify a fiber blend, film layer or carrying limit, and printed environmental wording is not material evidence.'),
('Arrange print on the usable panels','Place the logo and information around the opening, folds, seams and carrying features of the approved construction. Confirm the print route for the selected material rather than assuming a process used for another type of bag. Review color and fine text on a representative surface and check the design when the bag contains the intended item. Keep drawstrings, handles and attachment zones clear where the agreed manufacturing tolerances require it.'),
('Review the filled carrying experience','Pack the actual product and trial the intended opening, closing and carrying routine. Check insertion space, product movement, handle or cord access and the way the contents contact the base and seams. Agree checks for the loaded construction rather than for an unrelated empty reference. Record the material, size and feature set used in the review. Keep any later change to the product, handle, print or seam visible before confirming another order.')],
'roll-bag':[
('Specify bags and rolls as separate units','This reference shows bags supplied on rolls rather than a formed stand-up pouch. Define one bag by its width, length, opening and base-seal arrangement, then state the number of bags per roll. Confirm whether rolls have a core and how individual bags separate. Quantity should identify both the total bag count and the requested roll count. The catalog MOQ is expressed in pieces; confirm the conversion to rolls explicitly in the quotation.'),
('Review film, perforations and intended contents','Specify the actual film, thickness convention, seams and perforation position together. Describe the intended waste or storage task and any holder or dispenser used with the roll. Check the filled bag under the agreed load and handling conditions. A dark film color or a roll photograph does not establish puncture resistance, leak performance or environmental properties. Keep material statements tied to the agreed construction and any relevant supporting information.'),
('Confirm print and dispensing orientation','If printing is requested, identify the usable bag panels and the orientation when a bag is unrolled and opened. Keep artwork clear of perforations and seal areas. Confirm any repeating print layout with the actual bag pitch rather than using a flat pouch drawing. Review legibility and any required instructions at the finished size. Define the roll label and outer packing separately so the delivered count and product identification remain clear.'),
('Trial separation, opening and loaded handling','Use a representative roll in the intended holder or packing workflow. Check unrolling, separation at the perforation, opening the bag and access to its usable volume. Review base and side seams with the actual contents under agreed conditions. Record bag dimensions, roll count, core or coreless supply and export packing together. A change to film, bag pitch or perforation can affect dispensing, so confirm the revised sample before a repeat production run.')],
'reference':[
('Identify the component being requested','The image is a presentation or container reference rather than a confirmed flexible-pouch specification. State whether your project concerns a container, printed label, sleeve, wrap or another packaging accessory. Identify which pictured parts should be included and which are only styling or contents. Give the dimensions of the actual component and describe how it will be used. The quotation and production sample should name that component clearly before material or print choices are approved.'),
('Separate the supplied pack from the illustration','Review the container or presentation object separately from any printed layer and decorative accessory. A surface shown in a photograph does not identify its material, wall construction or manufacturing process. If a label or sleeve is needed for an existing object, provide that object or its dimensional drawing for fit review. Confirm the items to be supplied, their quantities and their own specifications rather than treating the whole staged image as one fixed catalog product.'),
('Prepare artwork for the confirmed surface','Once the component is identified, obtain its actual print area or production drawing. Confirm the artwork orientation, coverage and any restrictions around joins, curves or opening parts. For a separate label or wrap, check the dimensions on the real object before finalizing the design. Review the print and finish against a representative material surface. Keep the approved artwork associated with that component so an image or branding reference cannot substitute for its construction specification.'),
('Approve a sample for the defined component','Select a sample that answers the remaining questions about fit, assembly, access and printed appearance for the component being ordered. Use the actual companion object or contents where relevant and record which parts are included in the review. Confirm material and production availability in the written quotation. The image does not establish stock availability, a finished pouch format or the suitability of an unidentified material. Keep the sample, component drawing and approved scope together for ordering.')],
'sleeve':[
('Measure the pack being wrapped','A sleeve is a printed band or wrap around another product, tray or existing package. Measure that underlying object and confirm the wrap length, face panels, overlap and usable clearance. Define whether the sleeve slides on, folds around the pack or uses an agreed closing method. This reference should not be specified as a complete shipping box. Review the sleeve with the actual underlying pack so the drawing reflects its real shape and packing routine.'),
('Choose paper and closing details','Specify the paperboard or paper, thickness, grain and fold positions together with the closing method. Check that the sleeve can be fitted without damaging the underlying pack or slipping during the intended handling. If an adhesive area is requested, show it separately from the visible print. For food applications, confirm the role of the inner tray and any contact requirements rather than assuming the sleeve replaces a liner or directly contacting package.'),
('Plan readable panels and openings','Position the brand panel, product information and codes so they remain visible after the sleeve is fitted. Allow for corners, scores and the agreed overlap or seam. Review any window or cut-out against the actual tray or product underneath. Keep artwork clear of the closing area and verify the final orientation at the packing bench. Approve print and finishing on a suitable paper reference, then check readability on the assembled pack.'),
('Trial fitting, holding and removal','Fit the sample using the intended hand or machine process. Review sliding clearance, fold alignment, closing engagement and any window position. Inspect the complete pack through its expected display, handling and opening routine. Confirm which printed information stays with the product after the sleeve is removed. Record the underlying pack dimensions and the sleeve drawing together so a change to the tray, contents or outer package triggers the relevant fit review.')],
'dust-cover':[
('Fit the cover to the actual candle vessel','This reference is a removable printed dust cover for a candle presentation, rather than a folding carton or adhesive label. Confirm the vessel opening, cover diameter or outline, wick position and any cut-out needed for placement and removal. Review the cover with the actual vessel and candle configuration. A circular image alone does not define the final fit, paper thickness or how the cover will sit in the complete retail package.'),
('Review paper, cut-outs and handling','Choose the paper or card direction together with the cut shape and any tab, hole or slot. Check how the cover is placed at packing and lifted out by the recipient. Agree its role as a packaging component and confirm any necessary handling information with the candle owner. Printed instructions in a reference image are artwork to review, not automatically approved product information. Keep paper, dimensions and cut-out locations on the same specification.'),
('Prepare artwork at the finished size','Place the logo, product information and any approved wording around the actual wick and removal openings. Keep fine text and decorative details clear of cut and trimming allowances. Review requested foil or other finishing on the selected paper surface, including how it reads against the vessel and outer packaging. Check the final copy at actual size. The approved print file should identify both the cover outline and the information revision.'),
('Check placement and the complete presentation','Use a matching cover sample with the intended candle vessel and any outer box or wrap. Review alignment, access, surface contact and the packing sequence. Confirm which printed elements belong to the candle owner and which are only shown as design references. Record the approved cover material, shape and artwork together. If the vessel diameter, wick arrangement or packaging layout changes, review the affected dimensions and printed positions before repeating the order.')],
'bag-in-box':[
('Define the three-part packaging system','A bag-in-box reference brings together a liner, dispensing fitment and supporting outer box. Confirm whether the quotation covers one component or the complete set. Specify the intended beverage or other liquid, target fill volume, filling process and distribution conditions. Define the liner and fitment geometry before sizing the carton around the filled assembly. The photographed outer box does not establish the material, dimensions or performance of the liner hidden inside it.'),
('Review liner, fitting and carton together','Specify the liner construction, fitting position, cap or tap and compatibility with the filling equipment. Confirm the external box dimensions, board and access opening around the chosen assembly. Check how the filled liner is placed inside and how the dispensing component is positioned for use. Material and contact requirements should refer to the actual formulation and construction. Keep the liner and carton drawings linked so a component change is not made in isolation.'),
('Coordinate graphics and dispensing access','Plan the printed outer panels around the dispensing opening, carrying arrangement and product information. Keep instructions consistent with the actual fitment supplied. Identify any liner printing as a separate requirement and confirm its print area and process. Review the complete formed system for readability and access, including the box opening used to position the tap. A staged photograph is useful for presentation but cannot replace the final component drawings and approved operating information.'),
('Trial filling, assembly and dispensing','Fill a matching liner through the intended process and assemble it with the specified outer box. Review fitment positioning, filled support, access and dispensing under the conditions agreed for the project. Confirm the checks needed for the formulation, fill volume and delivery route. Record every component revision, the assembly method and what the quotation includes. Changes to the liner, tap, carton or filling conditions should be reviewed together before a repeat order is approved.')],
})

GUIDE_EXPANSIONS={
'packaging-quote-checklist':dict(
 answer='A useful packaging quote brief identifies the product, package format, dimensions, quantity per design, material direction, artwork and delivery country. Start with the information you know, label open decisions and ask suppliers to quote on the same basis. PackHubGlobe catalog products start at 500 pieces; splitting an order across sizes or artwork versions should be discussed explicitly.',
 example='Illustrative brief: a skincare brand needs 2,000 cartons in two artwork versions, plus a shared insert card. The buyer lists 1,000 cartons per version and a separate quantity for the card, describes the bottle with its cap attached, and identifies the delivery country. This makes the component quantities visible. It does not assume that all 2,000 cartons share one setup or that the card is automatically included in the carton price.',
 rows=[('Dimensions','State internal box size or the product dimensions to be fitted; add units and orientation.'),('Artwork versions','List the quantity for each printed design, colorway and size rather than only a grand total.'),('Commercial basis','Ask which items are included: samples, tooling, production, export packing and freight.')],
 mistakes=['Comparing quotations that use different materials or delivery terms.','Treating an image as a complete production specification.','Leaving artwork splits and delivery country out of the first enquiry.'],
 checks=['Product and complete component list','Quantity per size and printed design','Dimensions with units and measurement convention','Material and finishing priorities','Artwork status and sample decisions','Destination and target arrival window'],
 faq=[('Can I request a quote without a dieline?','Yes. Send product measurements or a sample and explain how the package will be used. A structural direction can be reviewed before final artwork is placed.'),('Does a 500-piece MOQ cover several designs?','List the quantity for each design. The catalog starting quantity does not mean different sizes and artworks can be combined automatically; ask for the proposed order split in the quotation.'),('What should I compare besides unit price?','Compare the same specification, quantity, sample scope, setup charges, packing and shipping basis. A lower unit price can describe a different package or exclude necessary work.')],links=['/catalog/paper-boxes/','/services/multi-product-sourcing/']),
'mailer-boxes-vs-shipping-boxes':dict(
 answer='Choose a mailer when the folding lid and opening sequence suit the order; choose a shipping-box direction when its closure, packing process and transport arrangement fit better. Both need the correct board, dimensions, inserts and validation for the contents. The format name alone does not establish parcel protection.',
 example='Illustrative comparison: an ecommerce candle brand ships one retail carton, a care card and protective internal packing. The buyer trials a folding mailer and a taped shipping carton around the same complete contents. The review records packing steps, product movement, label placement and the opening experience. The team compares finished parcel dimensions and agreed testing rather than assuming that the more decorative structure will also be the stronger shipping pack.',
 rows=[('Opening','Compare locking tabs or a folding lid with taped flaps and the desired return process.'),('Packing','Measure assembly time and accessibility using the same insert and loaded contents.'),('Shipment','Compare actual packed dimensions, weight, closure and the intended distribution conditions.')],
 mistakes=['Buying a mailer from exterior dimensions when product fit depends on usable interior space.','Testing the empty box without the retail pack or protective components.','Assuming a flute name or attractive print establishes transport performance.'],
 checks=['Complete packed product and weight','Internal fit and packing clearance','Insert or protective packing arrangement','Closure and return requirements','Courier label area and graphic priorities','Relevant sample and distribution checks'],
 faq=[('Can a mailer be used as the shipping package?','It can be reviewed for that role, but the finished loaded pack must suit the product and distribution conditions. Confirm board, closure and any protective components together.'),('Should the inside or outside be printed?','Consider what remains visible after shipping labels and tape are applied. Interior printing can provide a separate presentation area; compare the cost and print requirements of both surfaces.'),('Is a smaller box always better?','A close fit can reduce movement, but insufficient clearance can make packing difficult or transmit pressure to the contents. Review the full arrangement with a sample.')],links=['/catalog/mailer-shipping-boxes/','/services/quality-control/']),
'stand-up-vs-flat-bottom-pouches':dict(
 answer='Stand-up and flat-bottom pouches differ in base construction and presentation. Select the shape around product volume, filling workflow, closure access and shelf arrangement, then specify material and barrier requirements separately. Equal fill weights do not imply equal bag dimensions for products with different bulk density.',
 example='Illustrative comparison: a coffee buyer wants two pouch styles for the same bean fill. Both samples use the planned closure features and a representative product fill. The team compares front-panel visibility, headspace, standing behavior, zipper access and packing into an outer carton. The trial helps select a size and structure, but it does not establish shelf life or qualify an environmental claim for the final printed material.',
 rows=[('Filled shape','Compare the actual packed appearance rather than only a flat drawing.'),('Functional features','Identify zipper, valve, window or other features before artwork approval.'),('Material','Keep oxygen, moisture, light and seal requirements separate from the pouch silhouette.')],
 mistakes=['Choosing size only from a nominal gram capacity.','Selecting a metallic appearance as a substitute for a material specification.','Approving artwork before the gussets, seals and usable panels are confirmed.'],
 checks=['Product and target fill quantity','Approximate bulk volume or a representative fill','Material and shelf-life requirements','Closure, valve or window needs','Filling and sealing conditions','Finished size, panel layout and carton packing'],
 faq=[('Which format holds more product?','Capacity depends on finished dimensions, gussets, headspace and the product itself. Compare a filled sample of each proposed size.'),('Does flat-bottom construction provide better barrier?','Shape and barrier are separate decisions. Compare the actual material structures and relevant measured properties for the intended application.'),('Can one design work on both formats?','The graphic identity can be coordinated, but panels, gussets and seal areas may differ. Use a confirmed dieline for each structure.')],links=['/catalog/pouches/','/materials/barrier-films/']),
'folding-cartons-vs-rigid-boxes':dict(
 answer='Folding cartons and rigid boxes answer different presentation and packing needs. Compare product fit, opening, assembly, insert design, shipment volume and the whole order cost. Neither format automatically guarantees protection, and a rigid presentation box may still require a separate shipping arrangement.',
 example='Illustrative comparison: a beauty brand is preparing a single serum and a multi-item gift set. A folding carton is reviewed for the individual bottle, while a rigid format is reviewed for the complete set and its opening sequence. Both are sampled around the actual containers. The buyer compares the two order configurations, including inserts, packing labor and export cartons, instead of trying to make one box style serve every sales channel.',
 rows=[('Structure','Compare a formed carton blank with the proposed assembled or collapsible rigid construction.'),('Presentation','Review the opening sequence, insert retention and visible printed surfaces.'),('Logistics','Use finished export-carton dimensions and quantities to compare storage and freight inputs.')],
 mistakes=['Choosing from perceived thickness without reviewing structure.','Comparing an empty rigid shell with a complete carton-and-insert quote.','Assuming a premium appearance removes the need for transit protection.'],
 checks=['Retail product versus multi-item set','Internal dimensions and insert layout','Opening and product removal','Assembly and supply format','Print, wrap and finishing references','Outer shipment configuration'],
 faq=[('Can a rigid box ship flat?','Some collapsible constructions can be reviewed for flat supply. Confirm the actual construction, assembly steps and packed dimensions rather than assuming every rigid box collapses.'),('Is a folding carton suitable for a premium brand?','The result depends on structure, print, finish and fit. Review a representative sample against the desired customer experience.'),('Should a gift box include an insert?','Review product movement, presentation and removal with the actual set. The correct insert depends on the contents and the way the box will be handled.')],links=['/catalog/gift-boxes/','/catalog/cosmetic-packaging/']),
'internal-vs-external-box-dimensions':dict(
 answer='Internal dimensions describe the intended usable space; external dimensions describe the outside of the finished package. Specify which measurement you mean, the unit and the orientation. Include both when product fit and an outer shelf, carton or postal constraint must be satisfied at the same time.',
 example='Illustrative brief: a buyer measures a bottle at 50 × 50 × 140 mm, including the cap, and requests a carton with an insert. Those are product measurements, not an approved box size. The supplier reviews the clearance, insert and closure, then returns an identified dieline. The buyer packs the assembled sample and records usable interior measurements and the final exterior limits before approving the structure.',
 rows=[('Product dimensions','Measure every component that remains inside the pack, including protrusions and wrapping.'),('Internal dimensions','Define the usable space and the agreed length, width and depth orientation.'),('External dimensions','Record outer limits separately where shipping cartons, shelves or equipment impose constraints.')],
 mistakes=['Omitting units or reversing length and width between emails.','Adding an assumed universal board allowance to every structure.','Ignoring the space occupied by an insert, folds or closure parts.'],
 checks=['Actual product with cap and accessories','Labelled length/width/depth orientation','Internal or external convention','Units on every drawing','Insert, wrapping and packing clearance','Sample revision and controlled measurements'],
 faq=[('Can I calculate exterior size by adding twice the board thickness?','Do not use that as a universal rule. Folds, wraps, joints and construction can affect the relationship; confirm it on the actual structure.'),('Which size should be on my quote request?','Label what you know. Product dimensions can start a fit review; a confirmed internal box size can support a more defined request. Add any external limit separately.'),('Should I measure a flat box?','A blank can support manufacturing checks, while the assembled pack answers fit and use questions. Agree the measurement method for the dimension being controlled.')],links=['/services/structural-design/','/catalog/paper-boxes/']),
'packaging-print-proof':dict(
 answer='Approve the proof for the decision it can represent: layout, content, color, finishing or formed structure. Identify the material, artwork revision and print route. A screen image is useful for design discussion, but it cannot establish the physical appearance of every ink, foil, coating and substrate.',
 example='Illustrative approval: a brand reviews a carton with a large dark panel, a foil logo and small ingredient copy. The layout proof is used to check wording and position. A suitable physical reference is reviewed for the dark panel and foil appearance. The assembled sample is checked at folds and corners. Each approval is labelled, so approval of the layout is not later treated as approval of every production effect.',
 rows=[('Layout proof','Check wording, panel order, codes and positioning on the current dieline.'),('Color reference','Identify the material, output conditions and the colors that matter most.'),('Finished sample','Review the effects of folds, coating, foil, embossing and assembled surfaces.')],
 mistakes=['Approving color from an uncalibrated screenshot alone.','Sending finish layers without a clear legend.','Changing artwork after approval without identifying the new revision.'],
 checks=['Final copy and current dieline','Embedded or supplied artwork assets','Color priorities and reference','Separate white ink and finish layers','Actual-size text and code review','Named proof type and approval revision'],
 faq=[('Will the same CMYK values look identical on every material?','Do not assume that. Paper surface, film, ink coverage, output conditions and finishing influence the appearance. Review references for the actual components.'),('Does a foil logo need a separate layer?','Clearly identify the proposed foil area and its relationship to printed artwork. Confirm the required file setup with the prepress team.'),('What should I sign off?','Record the exact file, revision, proof type and decisions approved. List any effects or physical checks that remain open.')],links=['/services/artwork-preflight/','/materials/printing-finishes/']),
'packaging-sample-types':dict(
 answer='Choose a sample by the question you need it to answer. A stock sample helps screen a format; a structural sample checks fit and assembly; printed and production-representative samples address different appearance and manufacturing questions. State what remains unverified rather than calling every sample a final approval.',
 example='Illustrative plan: a brand first reviews a stock drawer box to understand the opening direction. A custom structural sample follows with the actual jewelry and insert layout. A printed reference then addresses the selected wrap and logo treatment. The buyer keeps a short approval record for each stage. This sequence separates useful early decisions from the checks that still depend on the final construction and artwork.',
 rows=[('Stock sample','Useful for screening a general direction; may not match final dimensions or print.'),('Structural sample','Useful for size, assembly and product access; does not automatically establish color.'),('Production reference','Agree the construction and processes it represents, plus any remaining validation.')],
 mistakes=['Using one sample to approve decisions it does not represent.','Testing a package without the actual accessories or final fill.','Failing to keep a revision record when several samples circulate.'],
 checks=['Question the sample must answer','Contents used in the evaluation','Material and construction represented','Artwork and dieline versions','Checks, findings and requested changes','Reference retained for production'],
 faq=[('Can I start with a stock sample?','Yes, when it helps answer an early question about format or material. Record where it differs from the intended order.'),('Does a printed sample prove transit protection?','A visual sample and a distribution evaluation answer different questions. Agree the relevant loaded-pack checks separately.'),('What happens after a sample change?','Identify the changed structure, material or artwork and decide which approvals need to be repeated. Do not mix references from different revisions.')],links=['/services/sampling/','/services/quality-control/']),
'label-roll-direction':dict(
 answer='Confirm label orientation using an unwind drawing that shows the roll, printed face, leading edge and direction of feed. Include core size, outer diameter, label dimensions and the applicator requirements. Supplier numbering systems can differ, so an unambiguous diagram is more useful than an unexplained direction number.',
 example='Illustrative order: a bottle brand has a portrait label and a machine that requires one particular edge to leave the roll first. The buyer supplies the applicator specification and marks the top of the artwork on an unwind drawing. A small application trial checks dispensing and orientation before the full order. The label is also read and scanned on the curved container, where its appearance differs from the flat approval image.',
 rows=[('Feed direction','Show which edge reaches the applicator first and how the printed face is oriented.'),('Roll geometry','Confirm core, maximum outside diameter, web width, pitch and gap.'),('Application','Check dispensing, alignment, detection and adhesion on the real container.')],
 mistakes=['Approving a direction number without a drawing.','Specifying the label size but omitting the liner and roll requirements.','Checking a code only on the backing sheet instead of the labelled product.'],
 checks=['Applicator or hand-application workflow','Label face and leading edge diagram','Core and outside diameter','Width, pitch, gap and liner','Container surface and application conditions','Trial result and approved roll drawing'],
 faq=[('Can a correct label print still be unusable?','Yes. Incorrect roll geometry or orientation can prevent the label from feeding as intended. Review print and supply format together.'),('Are unwind numbers universal?','Do not rely on that assumption. Confirm the meaning with a drawing and the equipment specification.'),('Should labels be tested on the container?','Yes. Container shape, surface and application conditions affect alignment, appearance and adhesion. Use representative finished products for the trial.')],links=['/catalog/stickers-labels/','/products/stickers-labels/roll-labels/']),
'paper-bag-load-testing':dict(
 answer='Review a paper bag as an assembled package carrying the intended contents. Specify the load, product shape, carrying routine and relevant conditions. Paper weight alone does not establish a finished-bag carrying limit; handles, attachment, seams, base and reinforcement all belong in the evaluation.',
 example='Illustrative evaluation: a retailer uses the same bag size for a soft garment and a boxed glass item. The buyer checks both filled arrangements because their shapes stress the bag differently. The review records handle comfort, product position, base behavior and the agreed carrying routine. The findings apply to the documented constructions and conditions; they are not turned into an unlimited weight guarantee for every product or environment.',
 rows=[('Contents','Record product weight, corners, contact points and the way items sit in the base.'),('Construction','Identify paper, handle attachment, reinforcement, seams and base folds.'),('Conditions','Agree carrying, repeated handling, storage and exposure relevant to the intended use.')],
 mistakes=['Using paper GSM as the only performance requirement.','Testing an empty bag or a different product arrangement.','Repeating a result after changing handles, adhesive or dimensions without review.'],
 checks=['Complete load and product arrangement','Handle and base construction','Paper and adhesive references','Intended carrying routine','Agreed conditions and acceptance criteria','Sample identity and findings'],
 faq=[('Can you quote a guaranteed load from a photograph?','A photograph does not establish the construction or test conditions. Share the intended load and use so a suitable sample evaluation can be planned.'),('Does a larger bag always carry more?','No general assumption should be made. Dimensions change the load path, base and handling, so review the complete construction.'),('When should the bag be checked again?','Review the evidence when relevant materials, handles, adhesive, size, load or use conditions change. Keep the new construction identifiable.')],links=['/catalog/paper-bags/','/services/quality-control/']),
'brand-color-across-materials':dict(
 answer='Coordinate brand color by agreeing material-specific references and reviewing the packaging range together. Coated board, kraft paper, clear film and printed labels can create different appearances from the same digital color values. Define the visual priorities and approval conditions before treating one component as the reference for every other one.',
 example='Illustrative range: a coffee brand combines a pouch, a kraft retail bag and a printed card. The buyer identifies the main brand color and reviews representative prints side by side. The pouch white-ink decision and the paper surface are discussed separately. The approval record names each material and artwork revision, making it clear that coordination across the set is the goal rather than an unsupported promise of identical appearance on every surface.',
 rows=[('Brand priority','Identify the critical logo or color area and the intended viewing context.'),('Substrate','Review the effect of paper shade, coating, clear film or metallic background.'),('Approval','Keep a material-specific print reference and review the coordinated set together.')],
 mistakes=['Sending a screen color as the only production reference.','Approving every component separately under unrelated lighting.','Ignoring white ink, coatings or the formed package when comparing the range.'],
 checks=['Critical brand colors and hierarchy','Each packaging material and print route','White-ink and finishing decisions','Suitable physical references','Agreed comparison conditions','Artwork versions and coordinated approval'],
 faq=[('Should every component use the same ink instruction?','The shared brand direction matters, but each material may need its own prepress and print decisions. Review the proposed route for every component.'),('Can a clear pouch match a paper carton?','A coordinated appearance can be reviewed, but the underlying surface and white ink need consideration. Compare representative samples together.'),('Which reference should be kept for repeat orders?','Keep identified references for each material and the agreed range relationship, together with the artwork versions. One loose sample without a specification is difficult to use consistently.')],links=['/materials/printing-finishes/','/services/multi-product-sourcing/'])
}

PROFILES['insert']=[
('Design the cavity around the actual product','Begin with the product, its dimensions and the orientation in which it will be packed. Identify the surfaces that can carry load and those that need clearance, including caps, decoration, controls and fragile edges. A cavity should locate the product while leaving a practical removal path. Agree which items are included in the set and how their support points relate to the outer box. Evaluate the filled arrangement rather than approving an empty tray by appearance alone.'),
('Confirm the molded material and geometry','Specify the paper furnish, forming route, wall geometry and surface requirements with the proposed manufacturing process. The photograph shows a shape and presentation direction; it does not establish fiber composition, recycled content or a transport rating. Discuss any dimensional variation that could affect a close-fitting product. Approve a representative sample for the actual contents and environment, including any contact-sensitive surface. Material or environmental claims need evidence for the agreed insert and intended destination.'),
('Review nesting and packing operations','Check how unfilled inserts nest, separate and move through the planned packing operation. Provide room for the operator to place and remove the product without forcing it against the cavity wall. Confirm the outer box dimensions, lid clearance and any separate wrapping as part of the same arrangement. If several insert designs share one outer box, identify them clearly so the correct product is matched to the correct cavity. Review packing quantity and shipping configuration separately from the finished set count.'),
('Approve the filled pack and controlled revision','Evaluate the insert together with the product and outer packaging under the handling conditions selected for the order. Inspect product movement, support, contact marks and ease of removal before agreeing the acceptance criteria. Tooling and cavity changes can affect fit elsewhere in the design, so keep the drawing and sample revision identifiable. Confirm any surface marking or decorative treatment through a suitable sample. A repeat order should refer to the approved product, insert and outer-pack configuration rather than only to the reference photograph.')]
